/* LearnSphere - PostgreSQL section quizzes.
   window.QUIZZES['pg:<sectionIndex>'] = {qs:[ ... ]}  (sectionIndex = position in the course list, starting at 0)
   Question:  {q, o:[options], a:<index> or [indexes], why, lec:<lecture index in that section>, x:1}
     - a with more than one index = "select all that apply"
     - x:1 marks a question on Additional content; it is used only when the learner ticks "Include additional content"
     - lec links the missed-question review back to the lecture
   Each quiz is a POOL, about twice the size of one attempt: write ~20 core questions and ~10 x questions per section.
   An attempt draws 10 core questions (and 5 x questions when the learner includes additional content); change this per section with
   nCore / nExt next to qs. Questions the learner has not seen yet are drawn first, so a retake brings new questions until the pool is used up.
   Add the next section's quiz as another assignment at the end of this file. Wording uses `code` and **bold** like the lessons. */
window.QUIZZES=window.QUIZZES||{};

/* ---------------- Section 1: Introduction ---------------- */
window.QUIZZES['pg:0']={qs:[
{q:'When `COMMIT` returns, the transaction survives a power cut. Which mechanism gives PostgreSQL this **durability**?',
 o:['MVCC keeps the old row version','The Write-Ahead Log is flushed to disk first','`VACUUM` copies the row to a second file','The shared buffers are written to disk every second'],
 a:1,why:'Durability comes from the Write-Ahead Log (WAL): the change is recorded in WAL on disk before the commit is acknowledged. Data pages can be written later and replayed from WAL after a crash.',lec:1},
{q:'Session 1 runs a long report. Session 2 updates a row the report will read. What happens in PostgreSQL?',
 o:['Session 2 waits until the report ends','Session 1 fails with a serialization error','Session 1 keeps seeing the old row version and nobody is blocked','The row is locked for reading until session 2 commits'],
 a:2,why:'MVCC: an `UPDATE` creates a new row version and keeps the old one for sessions whose snapshot needs it. The price is dead tuples that `VACUUM` must clean up later.',lec:1},
{q:'Which statements about a PostgreSQL **cluster** are true?',
 o:['It is one data directory (`PGDATA`) managed by one running server','It is a group of servers that work together','It can contain several databases','It has one port and one set of roles'],
 a:[0,2,3],why:'A database cluster is one data directory, with one port, one set of roles and one WAL stream, holding many databases. A group of servers is called a replication group or HA cluster.',lec:5},
{q:'At what level is an **extension** installed with `CREATE EXTENSION`?',
 o:['Per cluster, for all databases at once','Per database','Per schema only','Per role'],
 a:1,why:'Extensions are installed per database. Install it in every database that needs it (or in a template database). Some also need `shared_preload_libraries` and a restart.',lec:5},
{q:'You move a server from PostgreSQL 18.1 to 18.3. What is required?',
 o:['`pg_upgrade` and a new data directory','A dump and restore of every database','Replace the binaries and restart; the data directory stays as it is','Rebuild all indexes'],
 a:2,why:'18.1 to 18.3 is a **minor** release. A minor release never changes the on-disk format, so you stop the server, install the new binaries and start it again.',lec:3},
{q:'A cluster created by PostgreSQL 17 must run on 18. Which options work?',
 o:['Start 18 on the same data directory','`pg_upgrade` or dump and restore','Copy the 17 binaries over the 18 binaries','Change `PG_VERSION` to 18'],
 a:1,why:'A major version may change the data format and catalogs, so 18 cannot open a 17 cluster. Use `pg_upgrade` (in place or with a copy) or a logical dump and restore.',lec:3},
{q:'How long does the community support each major version after its first release?',
 o:['1 year','2 years','5 years','10 years'],
 a:2,why:'Each major version is supported for five years, then it reaches end of life and gets no more fixes. Plan the next major upgrade well before that date.',lec:3},
{q:'At 3 a.m. the `pg_wal` directory keeps growing. What is a typical cause the lecture gives?',
 o:['An abandoned replication slot holding WAL','`log_statement` is set to `none`','`autovacuum` is on','The database is too small'],
 a:0,why:'A replication slot makes the primary keep WAL until the consumer has read it. If the consumer is gone, WAL piles up until the disk is full. Find the slot in `pg_replication_slots` and drop it.',lec:2},
{q:'Which task belongs mainly to the **DBA**, as opposed to an application developer?',
 o:['Designing the tables for a new feature','Writing the application queries','Testing that backups can be restored','Choosing button colours'],
 a:2,why:'A developer designs tables and queries for a feature. A DBA runs the platform: configuration, security, recovery and performance across applications, including regular restore tests.',lec:2},
{q:'Which lab choice **must not** expose port 5432 to the internet?',
 o:['VirtualBox on a laptop with a host-only network','A cloud VM','A disposable container on localhost','WSL2'],
 a:1,why:'A cloud VM is reachable from the network by default. Open only the ports you need, restrict them to your own address, and never publish 5432 to the world.',lec:4},

{q:'What makes PostgreSQL **object-relational**?',
 o:['It stores Java objects directly','You can define your own types, operators, functions and index methods, and tables can inherit from other tables','It has no SQL interface','It only stores `jsonb` documents'],
 a:1,why:'Besides ordinary tables it supports user-defined types, operators, functions and index methods, table inheritance and rich built-in types such as `jsonb`, arrays, ranges and UUIDs.',lec:1},
{q:'Which extension adds support for geographic data?',
 o:['`pg_stat_statements`','`pgcrypto`','`PostGIS`','`postgres_fdw`'],
 a:2,why:'PostGIS adds geographic types and functions. `pg_stat_statements` records query statistics, `pgcrypto` adds encryption and `postgres_fdw` queries other databases.',lec:1},
{q:'A transfer debits account A, but the credit to account B fails, so the debit is undone as well. Which ACID property is this?',
 o:['Atomicity','Consistency','Isolation','Durability'],
 a:0,why:'Atomicity: all statements of a transaction succeed together or none of them take effect.',lec:1},
{q:'Dead row versions left by `UPDATE` and `DELETE` are cleaned up by which process?',
 o:['The checkpointer','`VACUUM` (usually run by autovacuum)','The WAL writer','The postmaster'],
 a:1,why:'MVCC keeps old row versions as dead tuples. `VACUUM`, normally run automatically by autovacuum, marks that space reusable.',lec:1},
{q:'Which of these are **proactive** DBA tasks? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['Capacity planning','Testing backups','Monitoring and patching','Freeing space after the disk has already filled up'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'Proactive work prevents problems: capacity planning, tested backups, patching and monitoring. Clearing a full disk is reactive work.',lec:2},
{q:'A developer asks for read-only access to the reporting tables. What is the best approach?',
 o:['Share the `postgres` superuser password','Create a role with only the needed `SELECT` privileges','Make the developer the owner of the database','Add the developer to `pg_hba.conf` with `trust` and no role'],
 a:1,why:'Least privilege: create a role with only the `GRANT`s that are needed. Superuser access or ownership gives far more than the task requires.',lec:2},
{q:'Which command shows the version of the **running server**?',
 o:['`psql --version`','`SHOW server_version;`','`pg_dump --version`','`ls /usr/bin`'],
 a:1,why:'`SHOW server_version` asks the running server. `psql --version` reports the client you are using, which may be a different version.',lec:3},
{q:'Which file in the data directory records the major version that created the cluster?',
 o:['`postgresql.conf`','`PG_VERSION`','`pg_hba.conf`','`postmaster.pid`'],
 a:1,why:'`$PGDATA/PG_VERSION` contains the major version of the cluster. Binaries of a different major version cannot open it.',lec:3},
{q:'Which `pg_dump` version should you use against a server?',
 o:['The same major version as the server, or newer','Any older version is fine','Always the oldest installed version','The version of the operating system'],
 a:0,why:'A `pg_dump` older than the server can fail or silently leave out features. Use the same major version or a newer one.',lec:3},
{q:'Which lab option hides the operating system, so it teaches little about `systemctl` and file permissions?',
 o:['A VirtualBox VM','A cloud VM','Containers (Docker or Podman)','A VMware VM'],
 a:2,why:'Containers are fast and disposable, but they hide the operating system, so you practise services, permissions and the firewall less. Use a full VM for those lessons.',lec:4},

/* additional content (x:1): used only when the learner includes additional content */
{x:1,q:'Which release introduced declarative partitioning, logical replication and the two-part version number?',
 o:['9.6','10','12','15'],
 a:1,why:'PostgreSQL 10 (2017) added declarative partitioning and logical replication, and switched from three-part (9.6.24) to two-part (10.1) version numbers.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'In which months do the scheduled **minor** releases arrive?',
 o:['January, April, July, October','February, May, August, November','March, June, September, December','Only in September'],
 a:1,why:'Minor releases come on the second Thursday of February, May, August and November. The November release is the last one for a branch that reaches end of life.',lec:13},
{x:1,q:'On a managed PostgreSQL service such as RDS, which tasks are **still yours**?',
 o:['Query tuning and indexing','Operating system patching','Verifying that restores work','Designing roles and privileges'],
 a:[0,2,3],why:'The provider takes over hardware and operating-system work, and automates many backups and minor updates. Query tuning, restore verification and privilege design stay with you.',lec:12},
{x:1,q:'The business says "we can lose at most 5 minutes of transactions". Which term is this?',
 o:['RTO','RPO','SLA penalty','MTTR'],
 a:1,why:'RPO (Recovery Point Objective) is how much data you may lose. RTO is how long recovery may take, and MTTR is the mean time to repair.',lec:9},
{x:1,q:'The documentation address ends in `/docs/current/`. Which version of the manual does it show?',
 o:['The version your server runs','The oldest supported version','The latest released major version','The development version'],
 a:2,why:'`current` always points at the latest release. Use the version selector (or `/docs/17/`) to read the page for your own server version.',lec:7},
{x:1,q:'What is the standard permission mode for the `PGDATA` directory?',
 o:['`0777`','`0644`','`0700`','`0600`'],
 a:2,why:'`0700` (`drwx------`): only the owner can read, write and enter. The server refuses to start if the permissions are too open. `0750` is also accepted.',lec:6},
{x:1,q:'Which contrib module lets you look inside `shared_buffers`?',
 o:['`pg_buffercache`','`pg_trgm`','`amcheck`','`pgcrypto`'],
 a:0,why:'`pg_buffercache` shows which pages are in shared buffers. `pg_trgm` is fuzzy text matching, `amcheck` verifies integrity and `pgcrypto` provides cryptographic functions.',lec:8},
{x:1,q:'According to the daily checklist, how much free space should the `PGDATA`, `pg_wal`, log and backup volumes keep?',
 o:['Any amount above 1 percent','More than 20 percent','Exactly 50 percent','Free space does not matter for WAL'],
 a:1,why:'The daily check is `df -h` with more than 20 percent free on each volume. A full `pg_wal` volume stops the database.',lec:9},
{x:1,q:'Which release added the `MERGE` command and stopped the `public` schema being writable by everyone?',
 o:['11','13','15','17'],
 a:2,why:'PostgreSQL 15 (2022) added `MERGE` and changed the default privileges on the `public` schema.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'Can you use a beta or release-candidate build of the next major version in production?',
 o:['Yes, once the first minor release is out','Yes, if you run `pg_upgrade` first','No, they are for testing only','Yes, on a standby only'],
 a:2,why:'Beta and release-candidate builds are for testing only. Never put them into production and never run `pg_upgrade` on live data with a pre-release.',lec:13}
]};

/* ---------------- Section 2: Installation Methods ---------------- */
window.QUIZZES['pg:1']={qs:[
{q:'"Installing PostgreSQL" is really three stages. Which stage creates the data directory with `template1`, `template0`, `postgres` and the system catalogs?',
 o:['Install (copy the program files)','Initialise (`initdb`)','Run (start the postmaster)','Verify (`SELECT version()`)'],
 a:1,why:'`initdb` creates the cluster: the data directory with the templates, the `postgres` database, the catalogs and the first WAL segment. Install copies binaries, and Run starts the server.',lec:0},
{q:'Which installation route does the course recommend for **production Linux** servers?',
 o:['Windows GUI installer','Package from a repository (`yum`/`dnf`, or RPM files)','Source build on every server','A container with no volume'],
 a:1,why:'Packages give easy patching, service files and repositories. Source builds mean you maintain everything yourself, and the GUI installer is for learning and development machines.',lec:0},
{q:'Which installation route needs careful **volume and backup planning**, because the container file system is temporary?',
 o:['Containers','The yum repository','The Windows GUI installer','RPM files'],
 a:0,why:'Containers are fast and reproducible, but data written inside the container disappears with it. Keep the database files in a volume and plan backups for it.',lec:0},
{q:'Which two build systems can build PostgreSQL from source?',
 o:['CMake and Bazel','Autoconf with `make`, and Meson','Gradle and Maven','Only `make`'],
 a:1,why:'PostgreSQL supports Autoconf + `make` (`./configure`) and Meson (`meson setup`, `ninja`).',lec:1},
{q:'Which `configure` option sets the install location, and what is its default?',
 o:['`--with-prefix`, default `/opt/pgsql`','`--prefix=PATH`, default `/usr/local/pgsql`','`--datadir`, default `/var/lib/pgsql`','`--home`, default `/home/postgres`'],
 a:1,why:'`--prefix=PATH` chooses where binaries and libraries are installed. The default is `/usr/local/pgsql`.',lec:1},
{q:'A source build has finished. What is missing that packages would have given you?',
 o:['The `psql` client','A service file: you must create a `systemd` unit yourself','The server binary','The `template1` database'],
 a:1,why:'Source builds do not install a service file. Create a `systemd` unit (the manual has an example) and set `PATH` and `PGDATA`.',lec:1},
{q:'You run `initdb` without choosing an authentication method. What does it warn about?',
 o:['The encoding is wrong','`trust` is enabled for local connections, so anyone who can reach the socket gets in without a password','The disk is too small','`wal_level` is too low'],
 a:1,why:'Without `--auth-local` and `--auth-host`, `initdb` enables `trust` and warns you. For a source build set the methods explicitly, for example `peer` locally and `scram-sha-256` over TCP.',lec:1},
{q:'For PostgreSQL 18 installed from the PGDG yum repository, where is the data directory by default?',
 o:['`/usr/pgsql-18/data`','`/var/lib/pgsql/18/data`','`/etc/postgresql/18/main`','`/usr/local/pgsql/data`'],
 a:1,why:'PGDG RPMs put binaries in `/usr/pgsql-18/bin` and the cluster in `/var/lib/pgsql/18/data`. `/etc/postgresql/18/main` is the Debian and Ubuntu layout.',lec:2},
{q:'Why do you run `dnf -qy module disable postgresql` before installing from PGDG on RHEL 8 or 9?',
 o:['It removes the old data directory','It stops the distribution module from hiding the PGDG packages','It starts the service at boot','It installs the contrib modules'],
 a:1,why:'The distribution has its own `postgresql` module. Disabling it makes `dnf` use the `postgresql18-*` packages from the PGDG repository.',lec:2},
{q:'In what order do you install the RPM packages by hand?',
 o:['server, client, libs','libs, client, server','client, server, libs','contrib, server, client'],
 a:1,why:'`postgresql18-libs` first (shared libraries), then `postgresql18` (client), then `postgresql18-server`. Then run the setup `initdb` and start the service.',lec:3},
{q:'The signature check on a downloaded RPM ends with `NOT OK` or `NOKEY`. What should you do?',
 o:['Install it anyway with `--force`','Do not install it: the file is damaged or altered, or the signing key is missing','Rename the file','Ignore it on air-gapped servers'],
 a:1,why:'A bad digest or missing key means you cannot trust the package. Import the PGDG key, re-download, and install only when the check says `digests signatures OK`.',lec:3},
{q:'Which are sensible choices in the Windows GUI installer? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['Keep port `5432` unless another server uses it','Use a strong password for `postgres` and store it safely','Put the data directory on a dedicated, backed-up volume','Install Stack Builder on every production server'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'Keep the default port, protect the superuser password and keep data on its own backed-up disk. Skip optional components such as Stack Builder on servers: fewer components means less to patch.',lec:4},
{q:'What must you do **before** removing PostgreSQL?',
 o:['Delete the data directory','Back up everything, for example with `pg_dumpall`','Reboot the server','Change the port to 5433'],
 a:1,why:'Uninstalling is a data-protection exercise. Check what is installed, take a full backup, stop the server, remove the software, and delete data only after proving the backup restores.',lec:5},
{q:'You run `dnf remove "postgresql18*"`. What happens to `/var/lib/pgsql/18`?',
 o:['It is deleted','It is kept','It is moved to `/backup`','It is compressed'],
 a:1,why:'Removing packages keeps the data directory. Delete it by hand, and only after you have confirmed that your backup restores.',lec:5},
{q:'On Debian or Ubuntu, `apt purge postgresql-18` does **not** remove a cluster you created. Which command does?',
 o:['`pg_dropcluster 18 main`','`pg_ctl destroy`','`dropdb main`','`rm /etc/postgresql`'],
 a:0,why:'On Debian and Ubuntu clusters are managed with `postgresql-common` tools. `pg_dropcluster 18 main` removes a cluster, so make a backup first.',lec:5},
{q:'On a new server `listen_addresses` has its default value. Who can connect?',
 o:['Only clients on the local machine (`localhost`)','Every address on the network','Only the `postgres` OS user','Nobody'],
 a:0,why:'The default is `localhost`, so remote clients cannot connect. Change `listen_addresses`, edit `pg_hba.conf`, and open the firewall deliberately.',lec:6},
{q:'In PostgreSQL 18, what is the default for data checksums on a new cluster?',
 o:['Off','On','Only on standbys','Only for system catalogs'],
 a:1,why:'From version 18 `initdb` enables data checksums by default. `SHOW data_checksums;` confirms it, and the checklist asks you to record it if you chose `off`.',lec:6},
{q:'What is `template0` for?',
 o:['It is the model copied by every `CREATE DATABASE`','A pristine copy used to create databases with another encoding or locale; connections are not allowed','The default database for administration tools','It holds the WAL'],
 a:1,why:'`template1` is the editable default model, `postgres` is the admin database, and `template0` stays untouched for creating databases with a different encoding or locale.',lec:8},
{q:'What is the default WAL segment size that `initdb --wal-segsize` can change?',
 o:['1 MB','16 MB','128 MB','1 GB'],
 a:1,why:'The first WAL file in `pg_wal/` is 16 MB by default. The size is chosen once, at `initdb` time.',lec:8},
{q:'What is the first step of the troubleshooting method for an installation problem?',
 o:['Reinstall the packages','Read the error message exactly and copy it; do not retry blindly','Disable the firewall and SELinux','Delete the data directory'],
 a:1,why:'Read the message, find the log (the first error is the useful one), classify the problem, change one thing, test, then write the cause and fix into your runbook.',lec:14},

/* additional content (x:1) */
{x:1,q:'After `apt install postgresql` on Debian or Ubuntu, what already exists?',
 o:['Nothing; you must run `initdb`','One cluster, created automatically, with configuration under `/etc/postgresql`','Two clusters on port 5432','Only the client tools'],
 a:1,why:'The Debian packages create a cluster for you, keep configuration in `/etc/postgresql/NN/main` and data in `/var/lib/postgresql`.',lec:9},
{x:1,q:'Which tool family manages several clusters side by side on Debian and Ubuntu?',
 o:['`postgresql-common` tools such as `pg_createcluster` and `pg_lsclusters`','`yum-utils`','`pg_basebackup`','`chkconfig`'],
 a:0,why:'`postgresql-common` provides `pg_lsclusters`, `pg_createcluster`, `pg_ctlcluster` and `pg_dropcluster`, plus wrappers that choose the right version.',lec:9},
{x:1,q:'Where must the database files live when you run PostgreSQL in a container?',
 o:['In the container image layer','In a volume that outlives the container','In `/tmp`','On the host `postgres` user home'],
 a:1,why:'A container file system is temporary. Mount a volume at the data directory so the cluster survives container removal and image upgrades.',lec:10},
{x:1,q:'Which things can systemd do for PostgreSQL? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['Start it at boot','Stop it at shutdown','Record its output in the journal','Take backups automatically'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'systemd starts and stops the service and records output in the journal. Backups are not part of the unit; use a backup tool and a scheduler.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'Installing an extension takes two steps. Which?',
 o:['Edit `pg_hba.conf`, then restart','Make the extension files available on the server, then run `CREATE EXTENSION` in each database that needs it','Run `initdb`, then `pg_upgrade`','Download a dump, then restore it'],
 a:1,why:'Files come from the package (contrib or a third-party package) or a build. `CREATE EXTENSION` then installs the objects, per database.',lec:12},
{x:1,q:'Why harden the server in the **first hour** after installation?',
 o:['Because PostgreSQL deletes defaults after an hour','Because no application depends on the server yet, so every change is easy','Because the license requires it','Because parameters reset after one hour'],
 a:1,why:'Defaults favour compatibility, not production. Setting passwords, authentication, logging, basic memory and a first backup is easiest before any application is connected.',lec:13},
{x:1,q:'How do you compile a PostgreSQL source tree that was set up with Meson in the `build` directory?',
 o:['`make -C build`','`meson compile -C build`','`./configure build`','`ninja install` only'],
 a:1,why:'`meson setup build ...` configures, `meson compile -C build` builds and `meson test` runs the regression tests. Installation is a separate step.',lec:15},
{x:1,q:'On a RHEL 8 or 9 style system, what must you do about the distribution `postgresql` module before installing PGDG packages?',
 o:['Enable it','Disable it','Nothing; it is ignored','Reinstall it with `rpm -e`'],
 a:1,why:'Disable it with `dnf -qy module disable postgresql` so that it cannot hide the packages from the PGDG repository.',lec:16},
{x:1,q:'On Windows, which accounts should have access to the data directory?',
 o:['Everyone','The service account and Administrators, and nobody else','Only the logged-in desktop user','Guests'],
 a:1,why:'The data directory must be readable and writable by the service account and Administrators only. Check with `icacls` and give the service account Full control after moving the directory.',lec:17},
{x:1,q:'`dnf` reports `GPG key retrieval failed` or `NOKEY` for a PGDG package. What is the usual fix?',
 o:['Import the repository signing key with `rpm --import`','Use `--nogpgcheck` permanently','Reinstall the operating system','Disable the firewall'],
 a:0,why:'The signing key is not imported. Import the PGDG key and retry. Skipping the check hides tampered or damaged packages.',lec:19}
]};

/* ---------------- Section 3: PostgreSQL Architecture ---------------- */
window.QUIZZES['pg:2']={qs:[
{q:'What is the role of the **postmaster**?',
 o:['It runs every SQL statement itself','It starts the server, accepts connections and hands each one to a dedicated backend; it does not do the query work','It writes all data pages to disk','It stores the WAL'],
 a:1,why:'The postmaster is the parent of all server processes: it reads the configuration, creates shared memory, starts the background processes and forks a backend per connection. It deliberately stays simple.',lec:0},
{q:'A server process dies abnormally. What does the postmaster do?',
 o:['Nothing; the other sessions carry on','It assumes shared memory may be damaged, terminates all sessions, re-initialises memory and runs recovery','It restarts only that one session','It shuts the server down permanently'],
 a:1,why:'Clients see "terminating connection because of crash of another server process" and can reconnect moments later, after crash recovery.',lec:0},
{q:'On start-up, what does the **startup process** do?',
 o:['Accepts client connections','Checks whether the last shutdown was clean and replays WAL if it was not','Runs `VACUUM`','Archives WAL segments'],
 a:1,why:'If the last shutdown was not clean, the startup process replays WAL from the last checkpoint (crash recovery). Until the database is consistent you see "the database system is starting up".',lec:0},
{q:'How does PostgreSQL serve a new client connection?',
 o:['One shared thread pool for all clients','The postmaster forks a new backend process for that connection','The client connects directly to the checkpointer','A background worker is reused'],
 a:1,why:'Process-per-connection: a dedicated backend is forked after the client connects and ends when the client disconnects.',lec:1},
{q:'What is the correct order of the five query stages?',
 o:['Planner, parser, analyzer, rewriter, executor','Parser, analyzer, rewriter, planner, executor','Analyzer, parser, executor, planner, rewriter','Parser, rewriter, executor, analyzer, planner'],
 a:1,why:'Parser (syntax), analyzer (objects and types), rewriter (views and rules), planner (cheapest plan from statistics), executor (runs the plan).',lec:1},
{q:'Which stage uses table statistics to estimate costs and pick between a full scan and an index?',
 o:['The parser','The analyzer','The planner','The rewriter'],
 a:2,why:'The planner compares possible plans, estimates their cost from statistics and picks the cheapest. `EXPLAIN` shows its choice.',lec:1},
{q:'Thousands of idle connections slow a server. What is the recommended remedy?',
 o:['Raise `shared_buffers`','Use a connection pool such as PgBouncer so a few dozen backends serve many clients','Add more WAL segments','Disable autovacuum'],
 a:1,why:'Each connection is an OS process with its own memory. A pool shares a small number of backends among many clients.',lec:1},
{q:'What does the **checkpointer** do?',
 o:['Copies WAL to an archive','Periodically writes all changed pages to the data files and records a checkpoint in WAL','Removes dead rows','Sends WAL to standbys'],
 a:1,why:'After a checkpoint, crash recovery only needs to replay WAL from that point. The archiver copies WAL, autovacuum removes dead rows and WAL senders stream to standbys.',lec:2},
{q:'What is the job of autovacuum?',
 o:['Encrypt data files','Remove dead row versions left by MVCC and update planner statistics','Flush WAL buffers','Terminate idle sessions'],
 a:1,why:'Without it tables bloat and the cluster eventually risks transaction ID wraparound.',lec:2},
{q:'Which parts live in **shared memory**? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['The buffer cache (`shared_buffers`)','WAL buffers','Lock tables and bookkeeping','`work_mem` sort areas'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'Shared memory is allocated once at start-up and used by every process. `work_mem`, `maintenance_work_mem` and `temp_buffers` are local to a backend.',lec:3},
{q:'What is the default page size PostgreSQL uses for tables and indexes?',
 o:['4 KB','8 KB','16 KB','1 MB'],
 a:1,why:'Tables and indexes are stored in fixed 8 KB pages, and the buffer manager caches those pages in `shared_buffers`.',lec:3},
{q:'`work_mem` is 4 MB. A query has three sort or hash steps and 100 connections run it at once. What is the worst case?',
 o:['4 MB','About 12 MB','About 1.2 GB','No limit applies'],
 a:2,why:'`work_mem` is granted per sort or hash operation, per query: 100 x 3 x 4 MB = 1.2 GB. Raise it per session for a heavy report rather than globally.',lec:3},
{q:'Roughly what share of RAM is a typical starting value for `shared_buffers`?',
 o:['About 1 percent','About a quarter','Almost all of it','Exactly 8 KB'],
 a:1,why:'PostgreSQL also relies on the operating system cache, so `shared_buffers` is usually around a quarter of RAM. `effective_cache_size` only tells the planner how much caching to assume.',lec:3},
{q:'What does the write-ahead rule say?',
 o:['Data pages are written before the log','The log record describing a change must reach disk before the changed data page does','Only committed rows are logged','WAL is written once a day'],
 a:1,why:'With the journal on disk first, a crash can be repaired by replaying it. Commit becomes one fast sequential write instead of many scattered page writes.',lec:4},
{q:'What is an **LSN**?',
 o:['A lock mode','A position in the WAL stream (Log Sequence Number)','A type of index','A replication slot name'],
 a:1,why:'The LSN is like a page number in the journal. WAL is stored in 16 MB segment files in `pg_wal`.',lec:4},
{q:'Why does PostgreSQL log the **whole page** after the first change following a checkpoint (`full_page_writes`)?',
 o:['To save disk space','To protect against partly written (torn) pages','To speed up queries','To make backups smaller'],
 a:1,why:'If the operating system writes only part of an 8 KB page during a crash, the full-page image in WAL repairs it during recovery.',lec:4},
{q:'When does the client receive "COMMIT" for an `UPDATE`?',
 o:['After the data page is written to the data file','After the WAL up to that point is flushed to disk','After autovacuum removes the old row','After the next checkpoint'],
 a:1,why:'Commit speed depends on the WAL flush. The dirty data page is written later by the background writer or checkpointer.',lec:5},
{q:'In `PGDATA`, how is a database folder under `base/` named?',
 o:['By the database name','By its OID (object ID)','By the owner','By the creation date'],
 a:1,why:'Each database is a folder named by its OID. Tables and indexes inside are files named by their relfilenode, split into 1 GB segments.',lec:6},
{q:'A single row is larger than an 8 KB page. How can PostgreSQL store it?',
 o:['It cannot','Very large column values move to a TOAST table','The row is split across two databases','The page size grows automatically'],
 a:1,why:'TOAST stores large values out of line, so one row can exceed the page size.',lec:6},
{q:'When is a transaction ID (xid) normally assigned?',
 o:['At `BEGIN`, always','Lazily, at the first write, so read-only work does not consume xids','At `COMMIT`','At connection time'],
 a:1,why:'Every transaction gets a cheap virtual ID, but the real 32-bit xid is assigned only when it first writes. That helps with transaction ID wraparound.',lec:10},
{q:'A session runs a plain `SELECT` while another session updates the same row. What happens?',
 o:['The `SELECT` waits for the `UPDATE`','The `SELECT` reads a row version from its snapshot and does not wait','The `UPDATE` waits for the `SELECT`','Both fail'],
 a:1,why:'MVCC lets readers and writers work on different row versions. Locks are needed for two writers on the same row and for DDL.',lec:10},

/* additional content (x:1) */
{x:1,q:'Normally, how many CPU cores can a single backend use for one query?',
 o:['One','All cores','Two','It depends on `shared_buffers`'],
 a:0,why:'A backend uses one core by default. Parallel query lets it call in helper processes for large read-only queries.',lec:7},
{x:1,q:'What does **JIT compilation** speed up?',
 o:['Commits','CPU-heavy expressions in big analytic queries','WAL archiving','Connection setup'],
 a:1,why:'JIT compiles expressions to machine code for long CPU-bound queries. It adds overhead, so it helps only for large analytic work.',lec:7},
{x:1,q:'What does MVCC keep so that readers and writers do not block each other?',
 o:['A lock on every row','Several versions of a row','A copy of the whole table','A separate WAL for readers'],
 a:1,why:'Instead of changing a row in place, PostgreSQL keeps several versions and decides which one each transaction may see. Cleanup of old versions is the job of `VACUUM`.',lec:8},
{x:1,q:'The planner chooses a plan by arithmetic on what?',
 o:['Estimates of rows and cost for each step','The size of the data directory','The number of connections','The WAL position'],
 a:0,why:'Most slow queries come from a plan that is wrong for the data because the estimates were off. Reading `EXPLAIN` and checking statistics finds the cause.',lec:9},
{x:1,q:'What are **timelines**?',
 o:['Separate histories of the database created after a recovery or promotion','The time zones of a cluster','Log rotation periods','Checkpoint intervals'],
 a:0,why:'After point-in-time recovery or a standby promotion PostgreSQL starts a new timeline so the new history cannot be confused with the old one.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'`pg_ctl` and `systemctl` control the server by doing what?',
 o:['Editing `postgresql.conf`','Sending signals to the postmaster','Calling SQL functions only','Restarting the operating system'],
 a:1,why:'They are convenient wrappers that send signals to the postmaster. For example a reload is the `SIGHUP` signal.',lec:12},
{x:1,q:'Which version of the frontend/backend protocol do clients speak today?',
 o:['Version 1','Version 2','Version 3','Version 7'],
 a:2,why:'Clients talk to the backend with a documented message protocol, version 3. It has a simple and an extended query style.',lec:13},
{x:1,q:'Which column of `pg_stat_activity` names every kind of server process?',
 o:['`state`','`backend_type`','`wait_event`','`application_name`'],
 a:1,why:'`backend_type` shows client backends and background processes such as the checkpointer, WAL writer and autovacuum workers.',lec:14},
{x:1,q:'Why does PostgreSQL use a **ring buffer** for large sequential scans?',
 o:['To compress data','To stop one huge scan from flushing the hot pages of everything else out of the cache','To encrypt the page','To reduce the WAL size'],
 a:1,why:'Large reads, vacuum and bulk writes use a small ring so the shared buffer cache keeps its useful contents.',lec:15},
{x:1,q:'Which is the **strongest** table-level lock mode?',
 o:['`ACCESS SHARE`','`ROW EXCLUSIVE`','`SHARE`','`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`'],
 a:3,why:'There are eight table lock modes from `ACCESS SHARE` (weakest, taken by `SELECT`) to `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` (strongest, taken by operations such as `DROP TABLE`).',lec:19}
]};

/* ---------------- Section 4: Basic Configuration and Connectivity ----------------
   This section has no Additional-content lectures, so the pool is 20 core questions and the include-additional toggle is hidden. */
window.QUIZZES['pg:3']={qs:[
{q:'For a remote client to reach a PostgreSQL server, which three layers must all allow the connection?',
 o:['DNS, DHCP and NTP','The network/firewall, the server listener (`listen_addresses`) and client authentication (`pg_hba.conf`)','`pg_dump`, `pg_restore` and `psql`','The WAL, the checkpointer and the postmaster'],
 a:1,why:'By default the server listens on localhost only. You must set `listen_addresses`, open the firewall for port 5432 and add a matching `pg_hba.conf` rule, then make sure the role has a password.',lec:0},
{q:'You changed `listen_addresses` and edited `pg_hba.conf`. What does each change need?',
 o:['Both need a restart','`listen_addresses` needs a restart; `pg_hba.conf` needs only a reload','`listen_addresses` needs a reload; `pg_hba.conf` needs a restart','Neither needs anything'],
 a:1,why:'`listen_addresses` and `port` are postmaster parameters, read at start-up. `pg_hba.conf` is re-read on reload (`pg_ctl reload` or `SELECT pg_reload_conf();`).',lec:0},
{q:'A remote client gets `no pg_hba.conf entry for host "x", user "u", database "d"`. What is the cause and fix?',
 o:['The password is wrong; reset it','No rule matches that client IP, user and database; add a `host` line and reload','The firewall is closed; open port 5432','`max_connections` is reached; raise it'],
 a:1,why:'The server log is precise where the client message is vague. This message means no rule matched: add a `host` rule and reload.',lec:0},
{q:'A remote connection attempt **times out**. What is the most likely cause?',
 o:['A wrong password','A firewall or network route problem','`wal_level` is too low','The `template1` database is missing'],
 a:1,why:'"Connection refused" points to the service being down or `listen_addresses` being `localhost`. A timeout points to a firewall or network route; open port 5432 and test with `telnet`.',lec:0},
{q:'The log shows `remaining connection slots are reserved`. What does that mean?',
 o:['`max_connections` has been reached','The disk is full','Autovacuum is stuck','The standby is behind'],
 a:0,why:'All ordinary connection slots are in use. Close idle sessions or put a connection pooler in front of the server.',lec:0},
{q:'Which `pg_hba.conf` line is dangerous on a reachable network?',
 o:['`host all all 10.0.0.0/24 scram-sha-256`','`host all all 0.0.0.0/0 trust`','`local all postgres peer`','`host appdb appuser 10.0.0.0/24 scram-sha-256`'],
 a:1,why:'`trust` accepts anyone without a password, and `0.0.0.0/0` means every address. Restrict the CIDR to your admin subnet and use `scram-sha-256`.',lec:0},
{q:'What must be **unique** for each cluster running on the same machine? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['The data directory','The port','The service name','The `postgres` OS user'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'Each cluster needs its own data directory, port and service. They can share the `postgres` OS user and the binaries.',lec:1},
{q:'A role exists in the cluster on port 5432, but a login to port 5433 says `role "x" does not exist`. Why?',
 o:['The role was deleted by autovacuum','Roles are cluster-wide; each cluster has its own role list','Port 5433 is blocked','The role name is case sensitive'],
 a:1,why:'Two clusters on one server have separate roles, passwords, `pg_hba.conf` files and tablespaces. When a login fails, first check you are connected to the intended port.',lec:1},
{q:'Two clusters of the **same major version** use the same tablespace `LOCATION`. What happens?',
 o:['Nothing; they share data safely','They write into the same `PG_18_<catalog version>` subdirectory and overwrite each other\'s files','The second cluster refuses to start','The tablespace becomes read-only'],
 a:1,why:'Never share a tablespace location between clusters. Use a naming convention that includes the cluster, for example one directory per cluster port.',lec:1},
{q:'What does a **fast** shutdown (`pg_ctl stop -m fast`) do?',
 o:['Waits for every session to disconnect','Rolls back active transactions, disconnects clients, then checkpoints','Aborts all processes without a checkpoint','Only stops new connections'],
 a:1,why:'Fast (`SIGINT`) ends sessions and writes a shutdown checkpoint, so the next start needs no recovery. Smart waits for clients; immediate aborts.',lec:2},
{q:'What is the default mode of `pg_ctl stop`?',
 o:['smart','fast','immediate','kill'],
 a:1,why:'Since PostgreSQL 9.5 the default is fast. Smart can wait forever on an idle but open session.',lec:2},
{q:'Which shutdown mode makes the next start perform **crash recovery**?',
 o:['smart','fast','immediate','All of them'],
 a:2,why:'Immediate (`SIGQUIT`) aborts every process with no checkpoint, so the next start replays WAL from the last checkpoint. Use it as a last resort.',lec:2},
{q:'Which parameter change needs a **restart**, not just a reload?',
 o:['`log_min_duration_statement`','`work_mem`','`shared_buffers`','`checkpoint_timeout`'],
 a:2,why:'`shared_buffers`, `max_connections` and `wal_level` have context `postmaster` and are read only at start-up. The others can be changed with a reload or per session.',lec:2},
{q:'Two `pg_hba.conf` lines both match a connection. Which one applies?',
 o:['The last matching line','The most specific line','The first matching line, reading top to bottom','Both are combined'],
 a:2,why:'Rules are checked top to bottom and the first match wins, so put specific rules above general ones.',lec:3},
{q:'The same parameter is set in `postgresql.conf` and in `postgresql.auto.conf`. Which value is used?',
 o:['The one in `postgresql.conf`','The one in `postgresql.auto.conf`, which is read last','The larger value','Neither; the server refuses to start'],
 a:1,why:'`ALTER SYSTEM` writes `postgresql.auto.conf`, which is read after `postgresql.conf`, so it overrides it. `pg_file_settings` shows every entry and which one applied.',lec:3},
{q:'Which view lists the rules of `pg_hba.conf` and also shows syntax errors in them?',
 o:['`pg_hba_file_rules`','`pg_roles`','`pg_stat_activity`','`pg_settings`'],
 a:0,why:'`SELECT * FROM pg_hba_file_rules;` is a safe way to check a rule file before you reload.',lec:3},
{q:'Which password method does the course recommend in `pg_hba.conf`?',
 o:['`trust`','`md5`','`scram-sha-256`','`password` (plain text)'],
 a:2,why:'SCRAM is the recommended password method. `md5` is legacy and deprecated, and `trust` is for labs only.',lec:3},
{q:'Which `psql` meta-command shows details of the current connection?',
 o:['`\\conninfo`','`\\dt`','`\\du`','`\\x`'],
 a:0,why:'`\\conninfo` shows the database, user, host and port. `\\dt` lists tables, `\\du` roles and `\\x` toggles expanded output.',lec:4},
{q:'You load a plain dump with `psql`. Which option makes it stop at the first error?',
 o:['`-q`','`-X`','`-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1`','`-o file`'],
 a:2,why:'Without `ON_ERROR_STOP`, `psql` carries on after an error. Add `-1` to run the whole script as one transaction, and `-X` to ignore `~/.psqlrc`.',lec:4},
{q:'You store a password in `~/.pgpass`. Which file mode is required?',
 o:['`0777`','`0644`','`0600`','`0700`'],
 a:2,why:'The file must be private (`0600`), in the format `host:port:db:user:password`. `psql` ignores it if other users can read it.',lec:4}
]};

/* ---------------- Section 5: Database and Storage Management ----------------
   Only one Additional lecture (Locale Providers), so the extra pool is 3 questions and nExt is 3. */
window.QUIZZES['pg:4']={nExt:3,qs:[
{q:'What is the difference between `pg_catalog` views and the `information_schema`?',
 o:['They are identical','`pg_catalog` is PostgreSQL-specific and complete; `information_schema` is SQL-standard and shows only objects the current user can access','`information_schema` holds the real tables','`pg_catalog` is only for superusers'],
 a:1,why:'Use `pg_catalog` for everyday administration and complete information. Use `information_schema` for portable queries that must also work on other databases.',lec:0},
{q:'Which statement about `pg_authid` and `pg_roles` is true?',
 o:['Both show password hashes','`pg_authid` also holds password hashes and is superuser-only; `pg_roles` hides them','`pg_roles` is per database','`pg_authid` only lists groups'],
 a:1,why:'`pg_roles` is the safe, readable view of roles. `pg_authid` is the underlying catalog and includes the password hashes.',lec:0},
{q:'You need a new database with a different encoding or locale than `template1`. Which template do you use?',
 o:['`template1`','`template0`','`postgres`','Any database with `IS_TEMPLATE`'],
 a:1,why:'`template0` is the pristine copy with `datallowconn = false`. Using it avoids inheriting the encoding and the changes you made in `template1`.',lec:1},
{q:'Can you change the encoding of an existing database?',
 o:['Yes, with `ALTER DATABASE ... ENCODING`','No; dump it, create a new database with the right settings and restore','Yes, but only to UTF8','Yes, with `VACUUM FULL`'],
 a:1,why:'Encoding and locale are fixed when the database is created. The only path is dump, create a new database with the correct encoding, and restore.',lec:8},
{q:'Why is `PGPASSWORD` discouraged?',
 o:['It is slower than `~/.pgpass`','The password can be visible to other processes on some systems; use `~/.pgpass` instead','It only works with `psql`','It is removed in version 18'],
 a:1,why:'Environment variables can leak. A `~/.pgpass` file with mode `0600` keeps the password private.',lec:2},
{q:'A `-h` option on the command line, a `PGHOST` variable and a compiled-in default all exist. Which wins?',
 o:['The default','`PGHOST`','The `-h` option on the command line','They are combined'],
 a:2,why:'Precedence: explicit command-line option, then the connection string or service, then the environment variable, then the compiled-in default.',lec:2},
{q:'Which objects are **cluster-wide** (shared by every database)? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['Roles','Tablespaces','The database list (`pg_database`)','Tables and indexes'],
 a:[0,1,2],why:'Roles, tablespaces and the database list are cluster-wide. Schemas, tables, indexes, functions and extensions belong to one database.',lec:3},
{q:'What is the difference between `pg_cancel_backend(pid)` and `pg_terminate_backend(pid)`?',
 o:['None','Cancel stops the current query and the session stays connected; terminate ends the whole session and rolls back its transaction','Cancel ends the session; terminate cancels the query','Cancel is for superusers only'],
 a:1,why:'`pg_cancel_backend` sends `SIGINT` and is the gentle option. `pg_terminate_backend` sends `SIGTERM` and disconnects the session.',lec:4},
{q:'Why must you **never** use `kill -9` on a backend process?',
 o:['It does not work on Linux','The postmaster treats it as a crash, disconnects everyone and runs crash recovery','It deletes the database','It needs a reboot'],
 a:1,why:'Abruptly killing a backend makes the postmaster assume shared memory may be damaged. Use `pg_terminate_backend` instead.',lec:4},
{q:'Why is the state `idle in transaction` dangerous?',
 o:['It uses 100 percent CPU','It holds locks and stops VACUUM from removing dead rows','It deletes the session','It blocks new connections only'],
 a:1,why:'An open transaction doing nothing keeps its snapshot and locks. Find it in `pg_stat_activity` and end it, and set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`.',lec:4},
{q:'What is the default `search_path`, and where is a new object created?',
 o:['`public` only; in `public`','`"$user", public`; in the first existing schema of the path','`pg_catalog`; in `pg_catalog`','`information_schema`; in the first schema alphabetically'],
 a:1,why:'The default path is a schema named after the current role, then `public`. `pg_catalog` is always searched first, implicitly.',lec:5},
{q:'From PostgreSQL 15, who may create objects in the `public` schema by default?',
 o:['Everyone','Only the database owner (through `pg_database_owner`)','Nobody','Only `postgres`'],
 a:1,why:'The default privileges on `public` were tightened in version 15, which protects against objects created by arbitrary users.',lec:5},
{q:'Which function gives the size of a table including TOAST and all its indexes?',
 o:['`pg_relation_size`','`pg_table_size`','`pg_total_relation_size`','`pg_indexes_size`'],
 a:2,why:'`pg_total_relation_size` = table + TOAST + indexes. `pg_relation_size` is the heap only, `pg_table_size` excludes indexes, `pg_indexes_size` is the indexes alone.',lec:6},
{q:'Does `\\dn+` show the **size** of a schema?',
 o:['Yes','No; it shows owner, privileges and description, so add up the relations with a query','Only for `public`','Only on version 18'],
 a:1,why:'To size a schema you must add up its relations, for example with `pg_total_relation_size` over `pg_class`.',lec:6},
{q:'Which contrib module must be preloaded through `shared_preload_libraries` (which needs a restart)?',
 o:['`pg_stat_statements`','`pgcrypto`','`pg_trgm`','`amcheck`'],
 a:0,why:'`pg_stat_statements` must be preloaded. Extensions are installed per database with `CREATE EXTENSION`; many, such as `pgcrypto` or `pg_trgm`, need no preload.',lec:7},
{q:'Which character encoding does the course recommend, and which should you avoid for new systems?',
 o:['`LATIN1`; avoid `UTF8`','`UTF8`; avoid `SQL_ASCII`','`SQL_ASCII`; avoid `UTF8`','`WIN1252`; avoid `LATIN1`'],
 a:1,why:'`UTF8` stores all of Unicode. `SQL_ASCII` does no encoding validation, so garbage bytes can be stored.',lec:8},
{q:'Under a non-`C` collation, how do you let `LIKE \'abc%\'` use a B-tree index?',
 o:['Create the index with `text_pattern_ops`','Raise `work_mem`','Run `VACUUM FULL`','Use `ILIKE` only'],
 a:0,why:'A plain B-tree supports prefix searches only under the `C` collation. With another locale create the index with `text_pattern_ops`.',lec:8},
{q:'When can an `UPDATE` be a **HOT update**?',
 o:['When it changes an indexed column','When no indexed column changes and there is room in the same page','Only on temporary tables','Only after `VACUUM FULL`'],
 a:1,why:'The new row version stays in the page without touching any index. A lower `fillfactor` (for example 80) leaves room for this.',lec:9},
{q:'What does plain `VACUUM` return to the operating system?',
 o:['All dead space','Only trailing empty pages; the rest becomes reusable inside the table','Nothing, ever','The indexes'],
 a:1,why:'`VACUUM FULL` rewrites the table and returns space but takes an exclusive lock. Plain `VACUUM` does not block reads or writes.',lec:9},
{q:'Which view lets you find **unused indexes**?',
 o:['`pg_stat_user_indexes` (scan counts)','`pg_stat_wal`','`pg_stat_replication`','`pg_stat_checkpointer`'],
 a:0,why:'`pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan` counts scans per index. The counters only grow, so compare readings over a period that includes your busiest jobs.',lec:10},

/* additional content (x:1) */
{x:1,q:'Which locale provider is the default, and why can it surprise you after an OS upgrade?',
 o:['`icu`; it is never versioned','`libc`; the ordering can change when the operating system or glibc is upgraded','`builtin`; it needs a restart','`libc`; it cannot sort text'],
 a:1,why:'The `libc` provider uses the operating system library. A different library version can sort differently and silently invalidate text indexes.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'What is the `builtin` locale provider (version 17 and later)?',
 o:['A provider built by PostgreSQL itself with simple, stable `C` and `C.UTF-8` behaviour','The operating system library','An external service','A provider only for Windows'],
 a:0,why:'`builtin` is implemented in PostgreSQL itself, so its ordering does not depend on the operating system.',lec:11},
{x:1,q:'What is a requirement for using the `icu` locale provider?',
 o:['PostgreSQL must be built with ICU support','The server must run on Windows','A superuser password','`wal_level = logical`'],
 a:0,why:'ICU is versioned and more consistent across platforms, but PostgreSQL must be built with ICU support.',lec:11}
]};

/* ---------------- Section 6: Logging and Parameters ----------------
   One Additional lecture (Parameter and Log Reference), so the extra pool is 3 questions and nExt is 3. */
window.QUIZZES['pg:5']={nExt:3,qs:[
{q:'With `logging_collector = off` (the upstream default), where do server messages go?',
 o:['Into `pg_wal`','To the stderr of the postmaster, and from there to wherever it was started (terminal, systemd journal or a file)','Nowhere','Into `postgresql.auto.conf`'],
 a:1,why:'Every process reports to the postmaster stderr. Turning on `logging_collector` captures those messages into log files with rotation.',lec:0},
{q:'What does GUC stand for in PostgreSQL?',
 o:['Global User Configuration','Grand Unified Configuration','General Utility Command','Generated Unit Counter'],
 a:1,why:'GUC is the name of the internal configuration subsystem, so parameters are also called settings or GUCs.',lec:1},
{q:'Which pg_settings column tells you **when** a parameter can be changed?',
 o:['`vartype`','`context`','`unit`','`category`'],
 a:1,why:'`context` (for example `postmaster`, `sighup`, `superuser`, `user`) decides whether a restart, a reload or a session `SET` is needed. `source` tells you where the current value came from.',lec:2},
{q:'`SELECT setting, unit FROM pg_settings WHERE name = \'shared_buffers\'` shows `16384` and `8kB`. What does that mean?',
 o:['16384 bytes','16384 pages of 8 kB, which is 128 MB','16384 MB','16 GB'],
 a:1,why:'`setting` is shown in the parameter\'s base unit, not with unit text. 16384 x 8 kB = 128 MB, the default.',lec:2},
{q:'What is the difference between a **reload** and a **restart**?',
 o:['A reload drops all connections','A reload re-reads the configuration and keeps connections; a restart stops and starts the server and drops them','They are the same','A restart keeps `shared_buffers` warm'],
 a:1,why:'A reload (`SIGHUP`) applies `sighup` parameters and `pg_hba.conf` without downtime. A restart is required for `postmaster` context parameters.',lec:3},
{q:'You ran `ALTER SYSTEM SET shared_buffers = \'8GB\'; SELECT pg_reload_conf();`. How do you see that a restart is still needed?',
 o:['`SHOW` already shows 8GB','`pg_settings.pending_restart` is true for that parameter','The server restarts itself','`pg_stat_activity` shows it'],
 a:1,why:'`shared_buffers` has context `postmaster`. A reload does not apply it; `pending_restart` flags the parameter until the server restarts.',lec:3},
{q:'Which parameter needs only a **reload** (or a session `SET`), not a restart?',
 o:['`shared_buffers`','`max_connections`','`wal_level`','`work_mem`'],
 a:3,why:'`work_mem` can be set per session or per role. `shared_buffers`, `max_connections` and `wal_level` size shared memory or start processes, so they need a restart.',lec:3},
{q:'What does `ALTER SYSTEM RESET ALL;` do?',
 o:['Resets every parameter in memory only','Removes every entry from `postgresql.auto.conf`','Deletes `postgresql.conf`','Restarts the server'],
 a:1,why:'It leaves `postgresql.auto.conf` empty. The values from `postgresql.conf` and defaults apply again after the next reload or restart.',lec:4},
{q:'Who may run `ALTER SYSTEM` by default, and how can this be delegated from PostgreSQL 15?',
 o:['Any user; it cannot be restricted','Superusers; `GRANT ALTER SYSTEM ON PARAMETER ...` delegates one parameter','Only the OS user `root`','Only replication roles'],
 a:1,why:'Since version 15 a superuser can grant `ALTER SYSTEM` on individual parameters to other roles.',lec:4},
{q:'`work_mem` is 4 MB by default. What does it apply to?',
 o:['The whole server','Each sort or hash node of a query, per query','Each connection, once','The WAL buffers'],
 a:1,why:'It is granted per sort or hash operation, so one query can use several times `work_mem`. Raise it per session or role for heavy reports.',lec:5},
{q:'What does `effective_cache_size` do?',
 o:['It allocates a cache of that size','It is only a planner estimate of the total cache available (PostgreSQL plus the OS); it allocates nothing','It sets the WAL cache','It limits temp files'],
 a:1,why:'A higher value makes the planner more willing to choose index scans. The memory itself comes from `shared_buffers` and the operating system cache.',lec:5},
{q:'Which are the valid values of `wal_level`, and which is the default?',
 o:['`minimal`, `replica`, `logical`; default `replica`','`off`, `on`, `full`; default `on`','`archive`, `hot_standby`; default `archive`','`low`, `high`; default `low`'],
 a:0,why:'`replica` supports archiving, `pg_basebackup` and streaming standbys. `logical` adds logical decoding. Changing `wal_level` needs a restart.',lec:6},
{q:'What is the default `checkpoint_timeout`?',
 o:['30 seconds','5 minutes','1 hour','1 day'],
 a:1,why:'Checkpoints start every 5 minutes or when WAL reaches `max_wal_size`. Production servers often raise the timeout and `max_wal_size` to spread writes.',lec:6},
{q:'What does `log_min_duration_statement = 0` do?',
 o:['Turns statement logging off','Logs every statement with its duration','Logs only errors','Logs statements shorter than a second'],
 a:1,why:'The default `-1` is off. A positive value logs statements that take at least that long, for example `1s`; `0` logs everything and can be very noisy.',lec:7},
{q:'Which are the values of `log_statement`, and what is the default?',
 o:['`none`, `ddl`, `mod`, `all`; default `none`','`off`, `on`; default `on`','`debug`, `info`, `error`; default `info`','`ddl`, `dml`; default `ddl`'],
 a:0,why:'`ddl` logs schema changes, `mod` adds INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and TRUNCATE, `all` logs everything. The default is `none`.',lec:7},
{q:'Which tasks does autovacuum perform? (Select all that apply.)',
 o:['Remove dead tuples and mark space reusable','Update the visibility map and free space map','Run `ANALYZE` to refresh statistics','Freeze old row versions to prevent transaction ID wraparound'],
 a:[0,1,2,3],why:'All four. It depends on `track_counts = on` (the default), and a launcher starts workers about every `autovacuum_naptime`.',lec:8},
{q:'`ALTER DATABASE appdb SET work_mem = \'16MB\'` and `ALTER ROLE report SET work_mem = \'256MB\'` both exist. The `report` role connects to `appdb`. Which value applies?',
 o:['16MB, the database default','256MB, because the role default has higher precedence','The server default 4MB','It is an error'],
 a:1,why:'Precedence from lowest to highest: default, `postgresql.conf`, `postgresql.auto.conf`, command line, database, role, role in database, connection options, session `SET`.',lec:9},
{q:'The log shows `checkpoints are occurring too frequently`. What is the usual action?',
 o:['Increase `max_wal_size`','Decrease `shared_buffers`','Turn off `fsync`','Increase `log_min_messages`'],
 a:0,why:'WAL is filling `max_wal_size` before `checkpoint_timeout` passes. A larger `max_wal_size` lets checkpoints be timed instead of forced.',lec:10},
{q:'What is `superuser_reserved_connections` (default 3) for?',
 o:['It limits how many superusers exist','It keeps slots free so a superuser can still connect when `max_connections` is exhausted','It reserves memory for superusers','It limits idle sessions'],
 a:1,why:'During a connection storm ordinary roles fill all slots. The reserved slots let the DBA log in and fix the problem.',lec:11},
{q:'The log shows `deadlock detected` again and again. What is the recommended fix?',
 o:['Raise `work_mem`','Make transactions touch rows in the same order','Turn on `fsync`','Increase `max_wal_size`'],
 a:1,why:'A deadlock is two transactions waiting for each other\'s rows. If every transaction locks rows in a consistent order, the cycle cannot form. Lock waits are logged with `log_lock_waits`.',lec:10},

/* additional content (x:1) */
{x:1,q:'What should you do with `fsync` on a production server holding real data?',
 o:['Turn it off to gain speed','Leave it `on` (the default)','Turn it off at night','Set it to `minimal`'],
 a:1,why:'`fsync` forces WAL and data to stable storage. Turning it off risks unrecoverable corruption after a crash.',lec:12},
{x:1,q:'Which log formats are structured and easy for tools to parse?',
 o:['`csvlog` and `jsonlog`','`htmllog` and `xmllog`','`binlog` only','`syslog` only'],
 a:0,why:'Besides plain `stderr` text, `log_destination` can write CSV and JSON logs, which log analysis tools can parse reliably.',lec:12},
{x:1,q:'What does `synchronous_commit` control?',
 o:['Whether a commit waits for its WAL to be flushed (and, with synchronous replication, replicated) before reporting success','The number of checkpoints','How many connections are allowed','The vacuum speed'],
 a:0,why:'It trades durability for latency. Lower settings can lose the most recent commits after a crash but never corrupt the database.',lec:12}
]};
